1
00:00:00,910 --> 00:00:02,890
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:03,670
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,170 --> 00:00:07,230
And my name is Kathryn and today we have a great lesson for you about traveling.

4
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:08,230
That's right.

5
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:20,950
You know, we have taken a look at previous lessons where you can buy a ticket, how to book a flight, what to do at the airport, but now we are going to see what we can do if our flight gets canceled.

6
00:00:21,510 --> 00:00:29,490
Okay, this is not a fun situation, but hopefully with our help you'll be able to understand what these messages are about flights that are canceled or delayed.

7
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:31,650
So let's take a listen to today's dialogue.

8
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:35,440
Good afternoon, sir.

9
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:40,530
May I please see your passport and reservation?

10
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:41,490
Here you go.

11
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:44,350
Oh, I'm sorry, sir.

12
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:47,930
This flight has been canceled due to some mechanical problems.

13
00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:48,890
Canceled?

14
00:00:49,170 --> 00:00:50,110
What am I supposed to do now?

15
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,540
We apologize for any inconveniences that may be caused by this.

16
00:00:55,160 --> 00:01:02,680
If your flight is urgent, I could put you on a waiting list for another flight this evening, but it's on a first come, first serve basis.

17
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:05,720
So there's no guarantee you'll be able to take that flight.

18
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:07,280
What are my other options?

19
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:15,100
Well, if you can wait until tomorrow, we will put you up in a hotel for today and you can take the scheduled flight for tomorrow morning.

20
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:16,140
That's fine.

21
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:16,920
I'll do that then.

22
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:18,660
Thank you for your understanding, sir.

23
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:20,080
I'll book your flight now.

24
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:24,040
All right, we're back.

25
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:27,050
So now it's time for us to go into Language Takeaway.

26
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:30,940
Language Takeaway.

27
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:36,920
All right, in Language Takeaway today, we have about four words or phrases.

28
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:40,300
The first one is the reason for this cancellation.

29
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,880
The reason is mechanical problems.

30
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:43,520
That's right.

31
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:48,060
She said that the airplane has some mechanical problems and that's why the flight is canceled.

32
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:49,780
So the key here is mechanical.

33
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:53,120
Mechanical meaning like related to machines, right?

34
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:58,800
All right, so something that's a mechanical problem has to do with the actual structure of the plane.

35
00:01:58,800 --> 00:01:58,940
That's right.

36
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:08,260
So for example, maybe something is wrong with the wings or maybe something is wrong with the engines, some type of problem that has to do with the mechanics of the machine.

37
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:09,680
Right.

38
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:15,260
And obviously, if you have a problem with the wings or the wheels or the engine, you don't want to fly that plane.

39
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:16,040
That's right.

40
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:19,560
So this caused the canceled flight.

41
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:23,980
Now after that, we heard this word, we apologize for any inconveniences.

42
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:25,680
What's an inconvenience, Marco?

43
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:26,220
That's right.

44
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:37,220
Well, obviously, I planned to take this flight, let's say Tuesday at 8 a.m. and if the flight is canceled, that means I have to wait an hour or three hours or maybe for the next day.

45
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:38,560
So it's an inconvenience.

46
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,880
It means it's not convenient for me.

47
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:42,240
It causes me some trouble.

48
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:47,320
All right, so something that is inconvenient causes problems or causes trouble.

49
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:47,960
That's right.

50
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:53,720
So for example, you have this late flight or canceled flight, that's an inconvenience.

51
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:56,040
But also, small things can be inconvenient as well.

52
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:59,160
Like I want to go home at 5.

53
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:01,070
Unfortunately, I have to write this report.

54
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:01,950
It's very inconvenient.

55
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:03,230
That's right.

56
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:10,010
So maybe you have a doctor's appointment tomorrow at 12, but you also have a meeting at your work so it's inconvenient, these two dates.

57
00:03:10,570 --> 00:03:12,670
All right, they cause problems for each other.

58
00:03:13,090 --> 00:03:18,010
So after that, the service person says if your flight is urgent, I can put you on a waiting list.

59
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:18,750
Right.

60
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:19,600
Waiting list.

61
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:23,460
So this is another important phrase to understand when we're talking about canceled flights.

62
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:24,160
That's right.

63
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:38,000
We usually get put on a waiting list and it's basically a list with everyone's names where you are waiting for the next available flight or you're waiting to be able to have the service provided.

64
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:45,430
All right, so you can have a waiting list at an airport like here, but you can also have a waiting list at a restaurant, right?

65
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:46,130
That's right.

66
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,370
Restaurants are super popular and you got to get on a waiting list.

67
00:03:49,670 --> 00:03:51,390
So can I put my name on the waiting list?

68
00:03:51,610 --> 00:03:54,450
That means can you call me when there's a free table?

69
00:03:54,790 --> 00:03:55,550
Right, exactly.

70
00:03:56,530 --> 00:04:05,490
So even though we are on this waiting list, it doesn't mean that we are guaranteed to be able to take this flight this evening.

71
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:06,810
So there is no guarantee.

72
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:16,750
All right, this guarantee, this is an important thing to talk about because a guarantee is something that means that we will definitely get a later flight.

73
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:17,770
Yeah, we promise.

74
00:04:18,170 --> 00:04:18,650
We promise.

75
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:22,400
So you often see the word guarantee in advertisements.

76
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:25,250
For example, this TV has a six month guarantee.

77
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:26,970
So we guarantee it won't break.

78
00:04:27,510 --> 00:04:29,190
If it does, we will fix it.

79
00:04:30,110 --> 00:04:32,210
But no guarantee means that we can't promise.

80
00:04:32,470 --> 00:04:33,290
We don't promise.

81
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:36,350
So it's possible that you won't get a flight.

82
00:04:36,790 --> 00:04:43,270
So for example, if you buy a new car, the manufacturer will guarantee that it's in good condition and it won't break down.

83
00:04:43,790 --> 00:04:48,930
But if you buy a used car, it's unlikely that they will give you that same guarantee.

84
00:04:49,110 --> 00:04:49,570
No, that's right.

85
00:04:49,730 --> 00:04:51,370
They cannot promise it because they don't know.

86
00:04:51,710 --> 00:04:51,870
Exactly.

87
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:54,530
All right, so a lot of words and phrases there.

88
00:04:54,670 --> 00:04:56,850
Why don't we go back and listen to our dialogue again.

89
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:03,430
Good afternoon, sir.

90
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:05,730
May I please see your passport and reservation?

91
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:06,670
Here you go.

92
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:09,540
Oh, I'm sorry, sir.

93
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:13,060
This flight has been canceled due to some mechanical problems.

94
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:14,400
Canceled?

95
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:15,300
What am I supposed to do now?

96
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:19,720
We apologize for any inconveniences that may be caused by this.

97
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:24,740
If your flight is urgent, I could put you on a waiting list for another flight this evening.

98
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:27,860
But it's on a first come first serve basis.

99
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,880
So there's no guarantee you'll be able to take that flight.

100
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:32,460
What are my other options?

101
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:37,300
Well, if you can wait until tomorrow, we will put you up in a hotel for today.

102
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:40,360
And you can take the scheduled flight for tomorrow morning.

103
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:41,300
That's fine.

104
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:43,080
I'll do that then.

105
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:43,840
Thank you for your understanding, sir.

106
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:45,260
I'll book your flight now.

107
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:56,240
All right, so now let's take a look at three key phrases that we have prepared for you on Fluency Builder.

108
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:56,520
Fluency Builder.

109
00:05:57,770 --> 00:06:01,330
Okay, so the first phrase we have here is a very important one.

110
00:06:01,450 --> 00:06:04,030
This tells us about the cause of the problem.

111
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:07,250
The service person said, I'm sorry, sir.

112
00:06:07,730 --> 00:06:11,530
This flight has been canceled due to some mechanical problems.

113
00:06:11,990 --> 00:06:12,190
Right.

114
00:06:12,690 --> 00:06:17,730
So this phrase due to basically is saying because of, right?

115
00:06:18,050 --> 00:06:18,290
That's right.

116
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:21,290
So this flight has been canceled because of mechanical problems.

117
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:24,440
And remember this due is D-U-E.

118
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:24,680
That's right.

119
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:26,440
D-U-E.

120
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:32,640
So I think it's kind of a nicer or more formal way of saying because of or the reason is.

121
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:34,160
Exactly.

122
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:39,520
So for example, the picnic has been canceled due to bad weather.

123
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:46,160
So it sounds a lot more professional, a lot more formal than if you say because of bad weather.

124
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:47,160
All right.

125
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,060
So just remember here due to indicates the cause or the reason.

126
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:50,900
It's like saying because.

127
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:52,320
All right.

128
00:06:52,470 --> 00:07:06,650
Now, when we were talking about the flights, we said that we are on the waiting list, but in order to catch this flight, they are going to take people on a first come first served basis.

129
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:07,560
All right.

130
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:09,280
First come first served.

131
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:13,680
Now, this is again a very common phrase.

132
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:16,260
It's related to that waiting list thing we were talking about.

133
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:21,020
If you have a waiting list, usually the waiting list is first come first serve.

134
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:27,080
So I could say, am I guaranteed a spot?

135
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:27,680
And you could say, well.

136
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:29,300
It's first come first serve.

137
00:07:29,560 --> 00:07:30,380
Maybe, maybe not.

138
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:31,800
Right.

139
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,700
If I'm number one on the list, I will probably get a seat.

140
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:37,050
But if I'm number 30, probably not.

141
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:41,070
So what we mean by it is that we don't give priority to anyone.

142
00:07:41,340 --> 00:07:43,650
We don't say, oh, you are more important or you're not.

143
00:07:43,850 --> 00:07:45,630
Whoever comes first gets it.

144
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:46,110
All right.

145
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:49,450
So if I come to the restaurant at five o'clock, I get to sit first.

146
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:51,370
But if you come at 510, you're next.

147
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:53,360
And after you, 530.

148
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:54,890
So it's first come first serve.

149
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:57,480
It depends on when you arrive.

150
00:07:57,480 --> 00:07:57,710
That's right.

151
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:00,310
So obviously this wasn't convenient for us.

152
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,910
So we are going to wait until tomorrow.

153
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:06,100
But the airline will put us up in a hotel.

154
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:08,060
We will put you up in a hotel.

155
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:09,160
All right.

156
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:12,140
So let's take this as a phrase because we all know what a hotel is.

157
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:14,600
It's a place where you stay when you're on holiday or business.

158
00:08:15,420 --> 00:08:18,620
But to put someone up, what exactly does this mean?

159
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:22,720
Basically you are paying for that person to stay at that hotel.

160
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:25,820
And you made the reservation and you're going to pay for the room.

161
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:31,020
So I will put you up means I will pay for your room at a hotel.

162
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:31,500
Right.

163
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,760
And it's usually only for accommodation for a hotel.

164
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,800
You can't say I will put you up for a meal.

165
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:38,080
No.

166
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:43,080
But you can say I'll put you up at my house, which means I will let you stay the night at my house.

167
00:08:43,660 --> 00:08:44,920
So not food, but actually sleeping.

168
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:46,560
Right.

169
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:47,860
So it usually involves sleeping.

170
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:49,640
That's right.

171
00:08:49,640 --> 00:08:54,980
So for example, you could say he's coming to our hometown for the wedding.

172
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:57,440
I will put him up at my house.

173
00:08:57,740 --> 00:08:57,880
Right.

174
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:03,740
Or if your company sent you to Japan, you say my company put me up at the Hyatt Hotel.

175
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:04,880
Oh, fancy.

176
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:05,880
Nice.

177
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:06,880
All right.

178
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:07,800
So that's all the phrases we have for you.

179
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:10,040
Let's listen to our dialogue one last time.

180
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:16,540
Good afternoon, sir.

181
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,820
May I please see your passport and reservation?

182
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:19,780
Here you go.

183
00:09:21,170 --> 00:09:22,670
Oh, I'm sorry, sir.

184
00:09:23,290 --> 00:09:26,250
This flight has been canceled due to some mechanical problems.

185
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:27,480
Canceled?

186
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:28,450
What am I supposed to do now?

187
00:09:28,990 --> 00:09:32,850
We apologize for any inconveniences that may be caused by this.

188
00:09:33,490 --> 00:09:37,870
If your flight is urgent, I could put you on a waiting list for another flight this evening.

189
00:09:38,450 --> 00:09:40,970
But it's on a first come, first serve basis.

190
00:09:41,230 --> 00:09:44,010
So there's no guarantee you'll be able to take that flight.

191
00:09:44,450 --> 00:09:45,550
What are my other options?

192
00:09:46,450 --> 00:09:50,410
Well, if you can wait until tomorrow, we will put you up in a hotel for today.

193
00:09:50,470 --> 00:09:53,450
And you can take the scheduled flight for tomorrow morning.

194
00:09:53,950 --> 00:09:54,430
That's fine.

195
00:09:54,710 --> 00:09:55,230
I'll do that then.

196
00:09:55,510 --> 00:09:56,990
Thank you for your understanding, sir.

197
00:09:57,450 --> 00:09:58,390
I'll book your flight now.

198
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:00,840
All right.

199
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:02,360
We're back.

200
00:10:02,540 --> 00:10:05,980
So talking about canceled flights, usually it's due to bad weather.

201
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:08,180
And usually you can catch another flight soon.

202
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:10,200
But sometimes you do have these problems.

203
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:19,220
Like when there was the whole 9-11 thing, I think a lot of flights were canceled or the volcano that erupted and European flights were grounded for a long time.

204
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:20,320
Yes, that's right.

205
00:10:20,580 --> 00:10:22,560
So sometimes there are natural disasters.

206
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:24,720
Sometimes there are man-made disasters.

207
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:32,320
But you actually said a very interesting word there and something that I think people who are learning about this should definitely study.

208
00:10:32,320 --> 00:10:33,320
The word is grounded.

209
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:35,080
A flight has been grounded.

210
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:36,120
Yes.

211
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:38,440
That means that the airplane will stay on the ground.

212
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:40,280
It means the plane will not fly.

213
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:41,280
OK.

214
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:52,960
So when planes are grounded because of bad weather or because of a natural disaster like a volcano, it's a very serious situation because the people who are going to fly can't now.

215
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:57,440
Now in our dialogue, we saw that they handled it, the airline handled it very, very well.

216
00:10:57,600 --> 00:10:59,340
But I think this is a very rare case.

217
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,400
You've had flights canceled before.

218
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:02,240
How did they handle it?

219
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:02,780
I have.

220
00:11:02,980 --> 00:11:06,340
I've had two or three flights canceled one time because of a blizzard.

221
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:08,160
So all flights were grounded.

222
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:10,000
The weather was very bad, very dangerous.

223
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:13,040
And the airline company said, well, we're sorry.

224
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:13,860
There are no more flights.

225
00:11:14,540 --> 00:11:16,160
But if you come back tomorrow, we can help you.

226
00:11:17,240 --> 00:11:20,680
And I was very lucky because they did not give me a hotel.

227
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:22,130
They did not put me up in a hotel.

228
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:23,770
But I was staying with a friend.

229
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:25,710
So my friends put me up at her house.

230
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:34,130
But the other time the flight was canceled, the airline put me on a flight from another company, another airline company.

231
00:11:34,520 --> 00:11:40,600
And so sometimes they can do that where they have a relationship with another airline company, they can do that.

232
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:42,990
So usually they do try to handle it relatively well.

233
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:50,400
But I've heard sometimes that they are not very understanding or very sympathetic to the inconvenience they cause.

234
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:51,060
Definitely.

235
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:55,480
And I think the worst is when it's bad weather because everyone's angry.

236
00:11:55,480 --> 00:11:59,100
And all the customers need to go somewhere, but they can't because there are no flights.

237
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:04,160
And so the people who are working for the airlines get very frustrated or upset.

238
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:04,720
Right.

239
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:06,540
Because basically it's not their fault.

240
00:12:06,740 --> 00:12:07,240
It's not their fault.

241
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:07,920
There's bad weather.

242
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:11,320
So there's not really much compensation the company can give you.

243
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:12,380
No, definitely not.

244
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:13,200
Not really their fault.

245
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:20,010
But it's hard not to get frustrated because you want to go home, you're tired, you don't want to pay for another night in a hotel.

246
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:22,510
So I can understand both sides.

247
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:23,800
Right.

